WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.293
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.293 --> 00:00:05.010
We're here at KubeCon Barcelona,
and with me is Vila Aikas.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.590
He is one of the most
humble engineers I know.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:11.670
He wants me to introduce
himself as a software engineer,

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.700
but he is co-founder, one
of the original co-founders

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.510
of Kubernetes, as well as
a co-founder of Knative.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.580
So let's start with Kubernetes.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.800
Can you tell me about how
it's evolved over time?

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.885
And this is probably one of the
biggest KubeCons we've been to.

00:00:26.885 --> 00:00:28.463
VILLE AIKAS: This is amazing.

00:00:28.463 --> 00:00:29.880
It's amazing how
it keeps growing.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.380
Every year, you
come in and you just

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.350
see how much it's growing,
how many new users are coming,

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.880
the solutions that people are
building on top of Kubernetes.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.425
It's just beyond wildest
dreams of when we started it.

00:00:41.425 --> 00:00:43.140
SAAD ALI: Yeah, it
is absolutely crazy.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.120
There are so many people.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.880
Now you've been involved
with a new project, Knative,

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.220
which has turned
into a hosted Google

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.080
solution called Cloud Run.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.240
Can you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:54.990
VILLE AIKAS: Sure.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.700
So Knative grew out
of the fact that we

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.740
were like, Kubernetes is
great, but it's really

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.790
hard for the developers to use.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:03.180
There's a lot of pieces you
need to understand about it.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.750
So I like to think of
it always as Kubernetes,

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.780
we did the cloud native
assembly language.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.010
And with the Knative,
we are building

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.470
tooling on top of it that
allows people to then build

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.130
cooler things on top of that.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:20.880
So it started out as an open
source product called Knative.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.690
It provides server-less
computing primitives

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:28.250
and building blocks that
you can go ahead and use

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.290
to build your own
software much easier,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.430
rather than having to deal with
the native primitive Kubernetes

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:35.760
resources.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.310
And it is also an API spec.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.640
So what you see is
on the Cloud Run,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.510
we were able to go ahead and
provide a hosted product, where

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:49.360
you can use the API without
not even having a Kubernetes

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.283
cluster to manage yourself.

00:01:51.283 --> 00:01:53.450
But we understand that
there's a lot of environments

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:56.850
where we're going to provide
these computing capabilities.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:01.140
So Cloud Run is one where you
don't even see any cluster.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:01.980
There is no cluster.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.810
It's the cluster-less compute.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.450
And then all the way
to your own host,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:07.950
if you have a
bare-metal Kubernetes,

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.330
you can run it on there.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.130
And we are seeing
more and more products

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.200
also being built
on top of Knative.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.400
So Cloud Run is one of them.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.460
There's others--
Pivotal, Red Pad.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.370
I think Rancher just launched
something yesterday or today.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.765
So it's grown as well.

00:02:23.765 --> 00:02:24.390
SAAD ALI: Nice.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.765
So what is the current status
of Knative and of Cloud Run?

00:02:27.765 --> 00:02:29.010
VILLE AIKAS: Sure.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.170
So we are-- on the
Knative side, we

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.910
are on a six-week
release schedule.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.750
So every six weeks, we
will cut a new release.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.250
We have a couple
of different stacks

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.720
there, so we want to make sure
that you can come in and either

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.440
take all of them, and I think
the term being described

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:47.540
is Voltron effect.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.140
When you use all
of them together,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.610
everything works much,
much, much better.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:54.360
But you can also go ahead
and come in and host only

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.900
the pieces that you care about.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.800
So some of those are serving
and eventing, also has build.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.470
So if you, for example,
have your own build system,

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:02.920
you're happy with that, great.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:03.510
We don't care.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.490
You can just use serving
or eventing itself.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.030
So what we have working
on the serving side

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.740
is really closely--

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.440
we are working very hard to
getting it to 1.0 release.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.050
We just dropped
two weeks ago 0.6,

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:20.305
and eventing is a
little bit behind.

00:03:20.305 --> 00:03:22.930
So we are going to go ahead and
release serving first, and then

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:23.460
eventing.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.830
But hopefully sometime
in the fall time period,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.745
we'll be in the 1.0 release.

00:03:27.745 --> 00:03:29.370
SAAD ALI: So as a
customer, why would I

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.540
choose to run on Cloud Run?

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:32.910
VILLE AIKAS: Ease of management.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.660
So you don't have to
care about-- you just

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.830
give us the code, and we run it
for you, scale it down to zero.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:42.000
So you truly get the server-less
benefits of scale to zero,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.550
pay only for what you use,
request base, and so forth.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.550
So on the Cloud
Run on the hosted,

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.480
you just give us the
code, we run it for you,

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.800
and you don't have to worry
about managing anything.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.660
You can also do this on GKE.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:58.330
So we have Cloud Run on GKE,
where if you have a cluster,

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:01.320
or you have other
computing needs that you

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.110
want to go and do on
GKE, then the great thing

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:08.230
is that you can just go and
install a Cloud Run GKE add-on.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.460
And then you can go and
still use the same API.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.120
So you can pick and choose.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.500
And then, of course, you
can install the open source

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.430
Knative yourself onto any
cluster, any Kubernetes

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:18.167
cluster.

00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:19.000
SAAD ALI: Very cool.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.340
So basically, you can use the
Knative API through Cloud Run.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.410
But if you want to drop
down into a lower level,

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.023
you can basically go and
access a GKE cluster directly.

00:04:28.023 --> 00:04:29.190
VILLE AIKAS: That's correct.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:29.760
Yup.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:30.970
SAAD ALI: Very cool.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.500
So where are we
going in the future?

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.410
What are you excited about next?

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.910
VILLE AIKAS: I'm excited about
a couple of different things.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:40.830
One of them is getting it
to GA, 1.0 release, where

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:44.260
we feel that it's ready to go.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.360
The second thing is
seeing the community grow.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.758
So again, kind of like what
we see with the Kubernetes

00:04:48.758 --> 00:04:51.300
in the early days, it's great
to go and come up with thoughts

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.638
and ideas and share them.

00:04:52.638 --> 00:04:54.930
But what's really interesting
is when people start then

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:57.420
building products on top
of that, like we've seen,

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.130
as well as the users.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.630
So we are seeing a
lot of developers

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.130
coming in and
joining the community

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:06.600
and providing a lot of great
ideas, great contributions.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.010
It's awesome.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:09.420
SAAD ALI: That is great.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.407
So last question.

00:05:10.407 --> 00:05:12.615
What are you looking forward
to at KubeCon this year?

00:05:12.615 --> 00:05:14.550
VILLE AIKAS: Just seeing
a lot of old friends,

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:17.760
seeing all the cool things
that are being built, and--

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.210
yeah, and just the community.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:20.340
It's just awesome.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:20.640
SAAD ALI: All right.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.880
Thank you so much for your time.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:22.260
Take care.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:23.552
VILLE AIKAS: Thank you so much.

00:05:23.552 --> 00:05:26.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

